ilippe Boule in this edition was clearly to push the game away from its routes in second edition <i>Vampire: the Masquerade</i>. The results are, I would argue, somewhat mixed. There are some clear successes, such as the re-imagining of Mortis, or the updating of the Road system into something more detailed and coherent. But the notion of High Clans and Low Clans doesn't really work, nor does the War of Princes (as presented clearly and painfully in <i>Under the Black Cross</i>, the worst chronicle without exception published by White Wolf). There are mechanical interactions which clearly have not been thoroughly considered. For instance, with weapons and combat there was no consideration paid to the addition of overage and division between bashing and lethal damage. The index is terrible (core rules are not referenced, but illustrations of signature characters are), and I am still resentful of the plagiarised map on the opening endpapers.<br /> <br /> The twentieth anniversary edition of <i>Vampire: the Dark Ages</i> fixed some things, and broke others. Overall, this presents the most functional and robust set of rules, a fairly mixed bag in terms of art (having it all in colour is a bit unforgiving in places), and an indicator that editing and development are important; from page to page you can go from well-written descriptions of the setting and rules, to paragraphs which are barely coherent. Clearly, the underlying quality is solely down to the strength of the original authors, which shows through in the final product. And there are some bizarre design choices - did anyone want the Ahrimanes, or the Niktuku as bloodlines?<br /> <br /> The chapter on Roads is a complete mess, and the weapons table only partly fixed, but then you have some very good guidance for Storytellers. And the book has a chapter on the medieval world which focuses on Italy and Eastern Europe, has multiple factual errors on most pages, and seems to think that places like Hungary are a mystery to players of the game.<br /> <br /> If only there was a good edition, to combine the strengths of the various editions, and to get rid of at least most of the obvious flaws.</div> </div> </div> </div>Thank you so much for this. This is a massive help. You\u2019ve managed to distill each edition into a paragraph, which couldn\u2019t have been easy.<br /> <br /> It appears there isn\u2019t an easy pick between the three. Until now I was leaning in the direction of using Vampire: The Dark Ages for setting and tone and Dark Ages: Vampire for the actual system. Now I\u2019m not so sure.<br /> Some further thought is in order.<br /> <br /> Many thanks once again.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I had a conscience once. Alas, I appear to have misplaced it.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\u2615","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1309-andreas-rayne","name":"Andreas Rayne","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1309_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"246"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-06T10:17:11-07:00","id":1538174,"title":"","content":"I also found Dark Ages Iberia, Constantinople by Night, and Dark Ages Europe quite useful sourcebooks and the lore I found as a excellent source of ideas.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;Power that needs to advertise itself is already losing&quot; Grand Admiral Thrawn <br /> <br /> &quot;Coronation Starscream? This is bad comedy!&quot; Galvatron","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27361-lysander","name":"Lysander","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27361_11.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3523"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-07T09:20:29-07:00","id":1538206,"title":"","content":"Given that I consider that Revisited is the best edition of Vampire of all I'm strongly suspecting that Dark Ages: Vampire will be the line that runs best with my taste for this game.<br /> <br /> But I will freely admit that I haven't done as extensive reading, discussion and comparison of the material to form a definitive opinion.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/970-gurkhal","name":"Gurkhal","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"863"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-07T13:31:18-07:00","id":1538212,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>James_Willoughby</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1538133-which-edition-of-dark-ages-is-your-favourite-and-why?p=1538158#post1538158\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The first edition of <i>Vampire: the Dark Ages</i> remains the most intellectually and aesthetically cohesive.</div> </div> </div> </div>Excellent post which summarizes much of what I believe. My only real complaint about the original VDA is I would have liked a more detailed comparison between how things are done in the modern Camarilla and how things were done pre-Camarilla. There are huge differences. While the books did cover this to some degree in the differing Traditions, in practice I've seen most STs run Dark Ages domains almost the same as they do to modern Princes, and that should not be the case. Both the Anarch Revolt and the establishment of the Camarilla should be seen as revolutionary in terms of how vampires interacted with each other, and unfortunately we didn't get that.<br /> <br /> I also would have liked a bit more fidelity to how earlier editions portrayed vampire society at this time (more lone vampires, more rural based) rather than the big city based chronicles of the modern age. The kind of anonymity modern cities provide with their large geography and millions of people is vastly different than a small neighborhood with some 10,000 people. More thought should have been put into that and how to still make a good game from that setting. The types of city settings most people like and play in are still doable in some cities of the era, but mostly not found in Europe but elsewhere.<br /> <br /> But in terms of aesthetics and feel of a medieval Europe beset by vampires, VDA really nailed it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>James_Willoughby</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1538133-which-edition-of-dark-ages-is-your-favourite-and-why?p=1538158#post1538158\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">There are some clear successes, such as the re-imagining of Mortis, or the updating of the Road system into something more detailed and coherent. But the notion of High Clans and Low Clans doesn't really work, nor does the War of Princes (as presented clearly and painfully in <i>Under the Black Cross</i>, the worst chronicle without exception published by White Wolf).</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't think those innovations worked out well. The distinction between the High Clans and Low Clans don't make sense for various reasons given the canonical history, and feels like an attempt to introduce the conflicts of the Anarchs into the setting at a time when that couldn't be a thing, and the War of Princes feels like an attempt to introduce sect wars at a time when there were no sects. In other words, just a tired rehash of the Revised era setting instead of dealing with the themes of a society blithely nearing the edge of ruin and revolution.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-07T18:09:18-07:00","id":1538219,"title":"","content":"DAV's indepth exploration of roads and their meaning and ideas made me really, really happy, as far as it went. That edition certainly had its highs and lows, but that part was an especially high high and justified the whole experience for me. No other edition, even of vampire overall really, attempted to go that deep into the notions and expressions and how to actually play various different ways of expressing vampire morality, human and otherwise. (And Modern Revised's attempts especially were mostly mocking of the player and kinda half assed even when they gave a book for the purpose. I say this as someone otherwise partial to Revised).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":12141,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12141-markk","user":"MarkK","date":". 07-07-2024, 10:05 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\u2615","count":2},{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12141-markk","name":"MarkK","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"493"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-08T20:38:38-07:00","id":1538282,"title":"","content":"The Road of Kings and Heaven were interesting alternatives to the road of humanity and this is coming from someone who isn't a big fan of paths/roads of enlightenment.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;Power that needs to advertise itself is already losing&quot; Grand Admiral Thrawn <br /> <br /> &quot;Coronation Starscream? This is bad comedy!&quot; Galvatron","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27361-lysander","name":"Lysander","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27361_11.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3523"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-08T20:44:00-07:00","id":1538283,"title":"","content":"Dark Ages: Vampire, without a doubt.<br /> <br /> The detailed setting and the systems were amazing, I truly enjoyed them. DAV introduced us (finally!) to Abyss Mysticism, something that was on the waiting for a long time back on those days. With the corebook, the ST Companion (which was extremely well done and VERY useful), both Players Guide and Dark Ages Europe you truly don't need nothing else.<br /> The one thing that I'll point out is that first edition had Constantinople by Night, probably one of the best Vampire books ever, so that might challenge a bit my decision.<br /> <br /> - Saga","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1594,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1594-saga","user":"Saga","date":". 07-08-2024, 11:51 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\ude00","count":2},{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1594-saga","name":"Saga","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1594_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"695"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-09T05:23:58-07:00","id":1538292,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saga</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1538133-which-edition-of-dark-ages-is-your-favourite-and-why?p=1538283#post1538283\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Dark Ages: Vampire, without a doubt.<br /> <br /> The detailed setting and the systems were amazing, I truly enjoyed them. DAV introduced us (finally!) to Abyss Mysticism, something that was on the waiting for a long time back on those days. With the corebook, the ST Companion (which was extremely well done and VERY useful), both Players Guide and Dark Ages Europe you truly don't need nothing else.<br /> The one thing that I'll point out is that first edition had Constantinople by Night, probably one of the best Vampire books ever, so that might challenge a bit my decision.<br /> <br /> - Saga</div> </div> </div> </div>Thank you very much.<br /> I\u2019m still leaning towards DA:V as the system with V:tDA as the setting. Then there\u2019s always the option of going full DA:V should I wish to move the date forward.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I had a conscience once. Alas, I appear to have misplaced it.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1309-andreas-rayne","name":"Andreas Rayne","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1309_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"246"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-09T18:58:37-07:00","id":1538337,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>James_Willoughby</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1538133-which-edition-of-dark-ages-is-your-favourite-and-why?p=1538158#post1538158\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The story goes that Mark Rein-Hagen wanted a vampiric <i>Dungeons &amp; Dragons</i>, whilst Jennifer Hartshorn, the developer, wanted a more historically accurate game. Hartshorn won out (though some of the prospective material on vampiric roses and fae cattle did appear in <i>The Book of Storyteller's Secrets</i>). I personally think that Hartshorn's focus on gothic horror (she had been the first, and best, developer of <i>Wraith: the Oblivion</i>), and the subsequent development work on the game's supplement's over the following couple of years made the game a success, and is the reason why we are still talking about it now.</div> </div> </div> </div>The original VTDA is my favorite version of Dark Ages, but dammit, now I wish we could've gotten Mark Rein-Hagen's vision.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I'm just a trans guy and gaming fan, unabashed hedonist and unusually upbeat nihilist, fairly laid back. He/Him.<br /> <br /> If everything is political, then nothing is!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25920-matt-corleone","name":"Matt Corleone","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25920_8.webp","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"393"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-09T20:27:49-07:00","id":1538340,"title":"","content":"It would have been interesting to see what his ideas for Dark Ages would have been.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;Power that needs to advertise itself is already losing&quot; Grand Admiral Thrawn <br /> <br /> &quot;Coronation Starscream? This is bad comedy!&quot; Galvatron","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27361-lysander","name":"Lysander","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27361_11.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3523"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-13T23:46:29-07:00","id":1538553,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lysander</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1538133-which-edition-of-dark-ages-is-your-favourite-and-why?p=1538340#post1538340\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It would have been interesting to see what his ideas for Dark Ages would have been.</div> </div> </div> </div>There are a lot of hints. As mentioned, quite a bit of original material was shuffled off into the <i>Book of Storyteller Secrets</i>, as was some of the more fantasy-themed art. And some of the original material remained in the core book in some slightly odd passages, such as the two-thirds of a page in the setting chapter set aside for discussion of labyrinths, or the half-hearted equipment list which appeared at the very back of the book.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/317-james_willoughby","name":"James_Willoughby","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar317_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"297"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-14T00:20:42-07:00","id":1538554,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MarkK</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1538133-which-edition-of-dark-ages-is-your-favourite-and-why?p=1538219#post1538219\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">DAV's indepth exploration of roads and their meaning and ideas made me really, really happy, as far as it went. That edition certainly had its highs and lows, but that part was an especially high high and justified the whole experience for me. No other edition, even of vampire overall really, attempted to go that deep into the notions and expressions and how to actually play various different ways of expressing vampire morality, human and otherwise. (And Modern Revised's attempts especially were mostly mocking of the player and kinda half assed even when they gave a book for the purpose. I say this as someone otherwise partial to Revised).</div> </div> </div> </div>It's worth commenting on what <i>Dark Ages: Vampire</i> did right on Roads, given the other issues of that edition. Notable points are:<ul><li><b>Providing valid choices for Cainite morality:</b> In <i>Vampire: the Masquerade</i> characters are either attached to their humanity, or else are a monster, guided by a Path. This is a a very conscious and thematic dichotomy for the modern version of the game, and to consciously move away from this becomes a point of distinction for why the medieval world is different.</li> <li><b>Giving space for context and mechanics:</b> Too often in the World of Darkness games you have a set of mechanics introduced which are supposedly central to the game, and are then covered in a couple of paragraphs. <i>Vampire: the Dark Ages</i> introduced eight Roads in two pages. <i>Dark Ages: Vampire</i>, by contrast, takes sixteen pages to introduce five Roads, with a double-page spread to present each major Road to the reader, all placed in a specific chapter. If you want to have something to function as a major piece of game background and significant mechanic, this is how to do it.</li> <li><b>The Path system:</b> Unlike <i>Vampire: the Masquerade</i>, <i>Dark Ages: Vampire</i> fixes what had previously been an ongoing problem with virtually identical moral codes by creating variant Paths within given Roads. It's a simple idea, but adds so much more coherence to their use as game mechanics, and their function as background detail.</li> <li><b>Universal Roads and Paths:</b> Various Roads were introduced in <i>Vampire: the Dark Ages</i> in quite specific contexts. So, pick up <i>Veil of Night</i> and you have the Road of the Community which is the Islamic version of the Road of Humanity. Is this a good idea? No, and <i>Dark Ages: Vampire</i> steps back from this, and universalises the various moral codes. The Road of the Community, for instance, becomes the Path of the Community within the Road of Humanity. This is better for players (offering more options) and better for avoiding the obvious cultural insensitivities.</li> <li><b>Convergent Hierarchies:</b> One notion very central to the Path system is that the followers of different Paths within a Road should agree on fundamental moral points, whilst disagreeing on less pressing moral issues. If you look at the Hierarchies of Sin for the various Paths, you will notice that major sins tend to be identical across the different Paths of a Road, whilst minor sins tend to be different.</li> </ul>Though there were a couple of details that were less than ideal&quot;<ul><li><b>The Road of Sin:</b> They tried, but the end result really wasn't great. Look, you don't need a special Road for antagonists, as you already have one in the Road of Kings.</li> <li><b>Auras:</b> This mechanic wasn't well considered. Firstly, the effect on the game is too limited for a core mechanic, as the majority (arguably the vast majority) of characters will hover between four and seven dots in a Road, and will never be affected by this mechanic. Secondly, they never fixed the issue of the noisy Cappadocians. Thirdly, if you introduce a mechanic like this, don't use a game term already in use within another more heavily used mechanic.</li> </ul>You would think that DAV20 would be able to spot and fix these, but no, the chapter on Roads looks like a messy first draft, which has not yet been through editing. It moves away from universal roads, and creates certain Hierarchies of Sin in which the sins are rather randomly switched around. <br /> <br /> Funnily enough, this then leads to attempts to revise and improve upon DAV20, and to finish the work of <i>Dark Ages: Vampire</i>, with a <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/486261/The-Road-to-Damnation?src=OPF\" target=\"_blank\">more consolidated set of rules and background for Roads and Paths</a>.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/317-james_willoughby","name":"James_Willoughby","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar317_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"297"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-14T11:31:01-07:00","id":1538573,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>James_Willoughby</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1538133-which-edition-of-dark-ages-is-your-favourite-and-why?p=1538554#post1538554\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> It's worth commenting on what <i>Dark Ages: Vampire</i> did right on Roads, given the other issues of that edition. Notable points are:<ul><li><b>Providing valid choices for Cainite morality:</b> In <i>Vampire: the Masquerade</i> characters are either attached to their humanity, or else are a monster, guided by a Path. This is a a very conscious and thematic dichotomy for the modern version of the game, and to consciously move away from this becomes a point of distinction for why the medieval world is different.</li> <li><b>Giving space for context and mechanics:</b> Too often in the World of Darkness games you have a set of mechanics introduced which are supposedly central to the game, and are then covered in a couple of paragraphs. <i>Vampire: the Dark Ages</i> introduced eight Roads in two pages. <i>Dark Ages: Vampire</i>, by contrast, takes sixteen pages to introduce five Roads, with a double-page spread to present each major Road to the reader, all placed in a specific chapter. If you want to have something to function as a major piece of game background and significant mechanic, this is how to do it.</li> <li><b>The Path system:</b> Unlike <i>Vampire: the Masquerade</i>, <i>Dark Ages: Vampire</i> fixes what had previously been an ongoing problem with virtually identical moral codes by creating variant Paths within given Roads. It's a simple idea, but adds so much more coherence to their use as game mechanics, and their function as background detail.</li> <li><b>Universal Roads and Paths:</b> Various Roads were introduced in <i>Vampire: the Dark Ages</i> in quite specific contexts. So, pick up <i>Veil of Night</i> and you have the Road of the Community which is the Islamic version of the Road of Humanity. Is this a good idea? No, and <i>Dark Ages: Vampire</i> steps back from this, and universalises the various moral codes. The Road of the Community, for instance, becomes the Path of the Community within the Road of Humanity. This is better for players (offering more options) and better for avoiding the obvious cultural insensitivities.</li> <li><b>Convergent Hierarchies:</b> One notion very central to the Path system is that the followers of different Paths within a Road should agree on fundamental moral points, whilst disagreeing on less pressing moral issues. If you look at the Hierarchies of Sin for the various Paths, you will notice that major sins tend to be identical across the different Paths of a Road, whilst minor sins tend to be different.</li> </ul>Though there were a couple of details that were less than ideal&quot;<ul><li><b>The Road of Sin:</b> They tried, but the end result really wasn't great. Look, you don't need a special Road for antagonists, as you already have one in the Road of Kings.</li> <li><b>Auras:</b> This mechanic wasn't well considered. Firstly, the effect on the game is too limited for a core mechanic, as the majority (arguably the vast majority) of characters will hover between four and seven dots in a Road, and will never be affected by this mechanic. Secondly, they never fixed the issue of the noisy Cappadocians. Thirdly, if you introduce a mechanic like this, don't use a game term already in use within another more heavily used mechanic.</li> </ul>You would think that DAV20 would be able to spot and fix these, but no, the chapter on Roads looks like a messy first draft, which has not yet been through editing. It moves away from universal roads, and creates certain Hierarchies of Sin in which the sins are rather randomly switched around.<br /> <br /> Funnily enough, this then leads to attempts to revise and improve upon DAV20, and to finish the work of <i>Dark Ages: Vampire</i>, with a <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/486261/The-Road-to-Damnation?src=OPF\" target=\"_blank\">more consolidated set of rules and background for Roads and Paths</a>.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think this is a very good analysis. I alwasy found Roads/Paths, even in Masquerade, to be one of the most interesting aspects of the game. It's very likely that most of the characters I developed for VtM and VDA over nearly 20 years (and I used to make characters even when I couldn't play them, just for fun) were not on Humanity.<br /> <br /> I will only add that I did like the idea of auras/bearings and hated how it was poorly executed. It caused rifts at my table in how a character should be presented, as I had a character with 10 in his Road (a Setite saint) designed to have a very noticeable guru-effect on people because I understood the aura not merely as a mechanical bonus (that's there to help translate objectively, through social rolls, the impact the aura has on people), but as a narrative effect, where vampires with low or high Road scores would stand out in social settings - and vampires would generally have an idea of why, and if they have enough knowledge or experience even be able to make educated guesses on a vampire's Road from the impression he gives. Because of how my ST handled social settings in general, that just didn't work out (he typically made humans easily subservient regardless of the vampire's degree of magnetism, while supernatural NPCs were mostly impossible to influence significantly except with Dominate - maybe).<br /> I agree that it is a rare thing too as most vampires have no aura modifier. If I were to narrate now, I would consider ditching the mechanic due to its rarity and how it makes vampires maybe a bit too spiritually oriented (where is the aura actually from? Why do vampires get bonuses for controlling the Beast, but humans don't for having high Humanity? It sounds like something that should be justified through something like the stories of the curses received by Caine). Also, social rolls being generally up to ST discretion and optional makes the mechanic too liable to irrelevance.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","name":"Hermeticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"431"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-20T05:29:18-07:00","id":1538943,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>James_Willoughby</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1538133-which-edition-of-dark-ages-is-your-favourite-and-why?p=1538554#post1538554\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> It's worth commenting on what <i>Dark Ages: Vampire</i> did right on Roads, given the other issues of that edition. Notable points are:<ul><li><b>Providing valid choices for Cainite morality:</b> In <i>Vampire: the Masquerade</i> characters are either attached to their humanity, or else are a monster, guided by a Path. This is a a very conscious and thematic dichotomy for the modern version of the game, and to consciously move away from this becomes a point of distinction for why the medieval world is different.</li> <li><b>Giving space for context and mechanics:</b> Too often in the World of Darkness games you have a set of mechanics introduced which are supposedly central to the game, and are then covered in a couple of paragraphs. <i>Vampire: the Dark Ages</i> introduced eight Roads in two pages. <i>Dark Ages: Vampire</i>, by contrast, takes sixteen pages to introduce five Roads, with a double-page spread to present each major Road to the reader, all placed in a specific chapter. If you want to have something to function as a major piece of game background and significant mechanic, this is how to do it.</li> <li><b>The Path system:</b> Unlike <i>Vampire: the Masquerade</i>, <i>Dark Ages: Vampire</i> fixes what had previously been an ongoing problem with virtually identical moral codes by creating variant Paths within given Roads. It's a simple idea, but adds so much more coherence to their use as game mechanics, and their function as background detail.</li> <li><b>Universal Roads and Paths:</b> Various Roads were introduced in <i>Vampire: the Dark Ages</i> in quite specific contexts. So, pick up <i>Veil of Night</i> and you have the Road of the Community which is the Islamic version of the Road of Humanity. Is this a good idea? No, and <i>Dark Ages: Vampire</i> steps back from this, and universalises the various moral codes. The Road of the Community, for instance, becomes the Path of the Community within the Road of Humanity. This is better for players (offering more options) and better for avoiding the obvious cultural insensitivities.</li> <li><b>Convergent Hierarchies:</b> One notion very central to the Path system is that the followers of different Paths within a Road should agree on fundamental moral points, whilst disagreeing on less pressing moral issues. If you look at the Hierarchies of Sin for the various Paths, you will notice that major sins tend to be identical across the different Paths of a Road, whilst minor sins tend to be different.</li> </ul>Though there were a couple of details that were less than ideal&quot;<ul><li><b>The Road of Sin:</b> They tried, but the end result really wasn't great. Look, you don't need a special Road for antagonists, as you already have one in the Road of Kings.</li> <li><b>Auras:</b> This mechanic wasn't well considered. Firstly, the effect on the game is too limited for a core mechanic, as the majority (arguably the vast majority) of characters will hover between four and seven dots in a Road, and will never be affected by this mechanic. Secondly, they never fixed the issue of the noisy Cappadocians. Thirdly, if you introduce a mechanic like this, don't use a game term already in use within another more heavily used mechanic.</li> </ul>You would think that DAV20 would be able to spot and fix these, but no, the chapter on Roads looks like a messy first draft, which has not yet been through editing. It moves away from universal roads, and creates certain Hierarchies of Sin in which the sins are rather randomly switched around.<br /> <br /> Funnily enough, this then leads to attempts to revise and improve upon DAV20, and to finish the work of <i>Dark Ages: Vampire</i>, with a <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/486261/The-Road-to-Damnation?src=OPF\" target=\"_blank\">more consolidated set of rules and background for Roads and Paths</a>.</div> </div> </div> </div>Wonderful analysis.<br /> <br /> On the point of DAV20, I think that is one of my main issues with the edition. A lot of change for change\u2019s sake, arbitrary switching of Hierarchies of Sin, Discipline powers etc.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I had a conscience once. Alas, I appear to have misplaced it.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1309-andreas-rayne","name":"Andreas Rayne","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1309_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"246"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-26T14:51:30-07:00","id":1539341,"title":"","content":"The one thing I don't get about the V20 Dark Ages updates is how the pagan paths (Path of Watchful Gods, Path of Eightfold Wheel, and presumably Road of the Aesirgard from Wolves of the Sea were it to get updated) were in the Road of Sin when both mechanically and thematically they fit better as older sub-sets of the Road of Heaven.<br /> <br /> IIRC, even the OG VTDA from the 90's implied these pagan paths existed as the Road of Light before the Road of Heaven was codified. I'm not sure where the Road of Light specifically was introduced in VTDA or if it was one of those bits of fanon that became so widespread it felt like canon.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I'm just a trans guy and gaming fan, unabashed hedonist and unusually upbeat nihilist, fairly laid back. He/Him.<br /> <br /> If everything is political, then nothing is!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25920-matt-corleone","name":"Matt Corleone","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25920_8.webp","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"393"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-26T18:23:02-07:00","id":1539351,"title":"","content":"I believe that the Path of the Watchful Gods and Path of the Eightfold Wheel were supposed to be Paths of the Road of Heaven, but the writing and editing of that chapter was so bad so who knows?<br /> <br /> It is, of course, a fundamentally bad idea basing Paths around specific religions, for what should be obvious reasons.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/317-james_willoughby","name":"James_Willoughby","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar317_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"297"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-26T19:13:58-07:00","id":1539355,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>James_Willoughby</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1538133-which-edition-of-dark-ages-is-your-favourite-and-why?p=1539351#post1539351\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It is, of course, a fundamentally bad idea basing Paths around specific religions, for what should be obvious reasons.</div> </div> </div> </div>Why, in your view? I see issues of representation which can be addressed by both consulting experts of those religons and allowing customisation options so that different takes on the same religion can be represented, and the issue that vampire religions aren't necessarily the same as the human ones. But otherwise, I find it would be quite easy to write customizable lists of sin taken from the individual religion and it would seem more realistic, at least to me, than the approximations required by the idealised paths like the Road of Heaven.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":16965,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","user":"Hermeticus","date":".  The quote was not working 07-26-2024, 10:20 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","name":"Hermeticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"431"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-26T19:26:24-07:00","id":1539358,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Hermeticus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1538133-which-edition-of-dark-ages-is-your-favourite-and-why?p=1539355#post1539355\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Why, in your view? I see issues of representation which can be addressed by both consulting experts of those religons and allowing customisation options so that different takes on the same religion can be represented, and the issue that vampire religions aren't necessarily the same as the human ones. But otherwise, I find it would be quite easy to write customizable lists of sin taken from the individual religion and it would seem more realistic, at least to me, than the approximations required by the idealised paths like the Road of Heaven.</div> </div> </div> </div>By customisation options I don't mean preset options like the Paths. I mean an explicit statement that the list of sin provided is purely a sample according to the best knowledge and imagination of the authors and those they have been able to consult. Given that religions straddle the realm between objective and subjective and the authors cannot be aware or understand everyone's perspective, players and STs should feel free to change the written Road or its sins however they prefer.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","name":"Hermeticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"431"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-27T15:45:12-07:00","id":1539396,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>James_Willoughby</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1538133-which-edition-of-dark-ages-is-your-favourite-and-why?p=1538554#post1538554\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><b>The Road of Sin:</b> They tried, but the end result really wasn't great. Look, you don't need a special Road for antagonists, as you already have one in the Road of Kings.</div> </div> </div> </div>I disagree with the idea that the Road of Kings makes the Road of Sin redundant as an &quot;Antagonist Road&quot;. Setting aside that the Road of Kings is meant to be LESS inherently villainous than comparable modern Paths, the Road of Kings lends itself to a very specific KIND of villain. That of the &quot;Lawful Evil&quot; despot. That kind of thing is not, and should not be, the totality of &quot;evil morality&quot;. There is legitimate use in having a character who builds their whole self-perception around vice or the like. A &quot;low&quot; villain, versus the &quot;high&quot; villain (yes, I know etymologically the latter is a contradiction in terms).<br /> <br /> Whether or not you think the Road of Sin was handled well, it does different things than the Road of Kings, and lends itself to different characters. So it's not good to remove one because the other exists. It's like saying, &quot;Why do we need Apples? We already have Oranges!&quot;","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}